<PackageManifest Version="2.0.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/vsx-schema/2011">
  <Metadata>
    <!-- Bump this on every build that is installed. Visual Studio keys upgrades on this version: while it stays the
         same it treats each VSIX as a different extension, leaves the older folders under
         %LOCALAPPDATA%\Microsoft\VisualStudio\<instance>\Extensions alone, and loads whichever it finds first. -->
    <Identity Id="LiteLLM.VisualStudio.7a065ef0-9e0d-4bbc-9c0f-06d899fdbbbb" Version="1.7.5" Language="en-US" Publisher="EsmaeilHatami" />
    <DisplayName>LiteLLM Chat for Visual Studio</DisplayName>
    <!-- The Publisher above is the name the extension list credits; this is what the details pane says about the work. It
         is plain sentences in the order someone meeting the extension would want to know things, and with no indentation
         on the continuation lines so that a reader which keeps the line breaks and one that collapses them both read
         cleanly. Bullet characters are avoided for the same reason: in a pane that collapses whitespace a list marker
         would run into the sentence before it. The schema caps this element at 1000 characters, so it is budgeted rather
         than free â€” the numbers are the feature list, and Detail belongs in the README the package ships. -->
    <Description xml:space="preserve">Chat with an OpenAI-compatible LiteLLM gateway or the Claude Code CLI in Visual Studio. LiteLLM runs the extension's agent loop; Claude hands each turn to the CLI.

Ask, Agent and Plan modes, streaming replies with reasoning, markdown and fenced code with Copy Code, clickable file and C# symbol links, and VS Code-style context: the active file follows the editor, and @, # and / attach files, MCP tools and server prompts.

Agent edits the solution with optional diff confirmation and Undo, verifies the build, and optionally runs the tests. Conversations, retry, cancellation and a per-chat ignore-earlier-turns switch are local; token usage comes from each server's report.

It is also an MCP client over stdio, Streamable HTTP and HTTP+SSE, with per-call approval, a tool browser and OAuth. Servers start only when enabled.

Settings live in Tools &gt; Options &gt; LiteLLM and the Dashboard. API keys and MCP secrets are encrypted per Windows user with DPAPI. The UI follows the IDE theme.</Description>
    <!-- The mark the chat's header and its empty state draw, on the accent the shell pairs it with. Regenerate it with
         tools\make-icon.ps1 rather than editing the PNG: the geometry is in that script. The element sits here rather
         than beside DisplayName because the schema fixes this group's order: Identity, DisplayName, Description,
         MoreInfo, License, GettingStartedGuide, ReleaseNotes, Icon, PreviewImage, Tags. -->
    <License>LICENSE.txt</License>
    <Icon>Resources\Icon.png</Icon>
    <Tags>AI,LiteLLM,Chat,OpenAI,LLM,Markdown,Agent,Copilot,MCP,Claude</Tags>
  </Metadata>
  <Installation>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Community" Version="[17.0,19.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Pro" Version="[17.0,19.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
    <InstallationTarget Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Enterprise" Version="[17.0,19.0)">
      <ProductArchitecture>amd64</ProductArchitecture>
    </InstallationTarget>
  </Installation>
  <Dependencies>
    <Dependency Id="Microsoft.Framework.NDP" DisplayName=".NET Framework" Version="[4.8,)" />
  </Dependencies>
  <Prerequisites>
    <Prerequisite Id="Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.CoreEditor" Version="[17.0,19.0)" DisplayName="Visual Studio core editor" />
  </Prerequisites>
  <Assets>
    <Asset Type="Microsoft.VisualStudio.VsPackage" Path="LiteLLM.VisualStudio.pkgdef" />
  </Assets>
</PackageManifest>